{"step_index":1930,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:23:30Z","thinking":"The plan is to integrate an `EXPLOIT_DATABASE` directly following the `CWE_DATABASE`, populated with detailed CVE entries. Initially, an entry for \"CVE-2021-44228\" is added, providing crucial information like the vulnerability's title, affected service, version range, CVSS score, and associated CWEs along with its attack vector.\n\n\nA new entry for \"CVE-2021-44228\" related to Log4j is added, detailing the vulnerability's title, service, affected versions, CVSS score, CWEs, attack vector, and remediation steps. Additional entries for \"CVE-2017-0144\" (EternalBlue), \"CVE-2022-22965\" (Spring4Shell), and \"CVE-2019-0708\" (BlueKeep) will follow a similar format. Each entry will provide key details for understanding and addressing the respective vulnerabilities.\n\n\nAdding entries for CVE-2017-0144 (EternalBlue) and CVE-2022-22965 (Spring4Shell) confirms the established format of CVE details including title, affected service, version range, CVSS score, CWEs, attack vector, and remediation steps.  The new CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep) entry follows the established data structure, too.  A UAF in `termdd.sys` (CVE-2023-36889), and OpenSSL's Heartbleed (CVE-2014-0160) vulnerabilities are added.  Apache vulnerabilities (CVE-2021-41773, CVE-2021-42013, CVE-2017-5638) are included also.\n\n\nAdditional CVE entries are added for Apache Struts (CVE-2017-5638), Sudo (CVE-2021-3156), Polkit (CVE-2021-4034), and Dirty COW (CVE-2016-5195). The consistent data format includes title, service, affected versions, CVSS score, CWEs, attack vector, and remediation steps.\n\n\nCVE-2016-5195 (Dirty COW) and four new CVEs (CVE-2022-0847, CVE-2021-26855, CVE-2022-26134) are integrated, confirming that the current process follows the established format. Each entry incorporates title, service, affected versions, CVSS score, CWEs, attack vector, and remediation. The consistent structure ensures all necessary information is captured for each vulnerability.\n\n\nFour new CVE entries are added: CVE-2023-34362 (MOVEit Transfer SQL Injection), CVE-2024-23897 (Jenkins CLI Arbitrary File Read), CVE-2024-6387 (regreSSHion - OpenSSH RCE), and CVE-2023-22515 (Confluence). Each entry maintains the consistent format including title, affected service, version details, CVSS score, CWE, attack vector, and remediation steps. The OpenSSH vulnerability, identified by CVE-2024-6387, details a race condition affecting versions up to 9.8p1.\n\n\nAdded CVE entries for Zerologon (CVE-2020-1472) and PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-34527), following the established data format. Each includes title, affected service, version range, CVSS score, CWEs, attack vector, and remediation steps.\n\n\nA \"recon\" tool is registered to perform host and port discovery, employing Nmap if available, and a fast socket scanner as a fallback.  The \"exploit_db\" tool facilitates querying the vulnerability database.\n\nA \"generate_payload\" tool is added to create various security audit payloads.  A \"web_attack\" tool is registered for web attack surface assessment, targeting URLs for exposed endpoints, sensitive files, and security header checks.\n\n\nRegistered a \"post_exploit\" tool for post-exploitation activities, including privilege escalation and auditing, for Linux, Windows, and Active Directory environments. The tool's capabilities encompass enumeration checklists, persistence detection rules, and lateral movement audits. I will now examine the first tool's implementation.\n\n\nInitial port scan targets are defined, using a pre-defined common ports list. Command-line arguments provide the capability to refine the target ports, accepting ranges and comma-separated lists. If Nmap is installed, it becomes the primary method for port scanning.\n\nNmap is configured with service version detection and other settings. The first 100 ports from the user input are used for scanning, which is controlled by the port argument or default settings. The `subprocess.run` command executes the Nmap scan.\n\n\nIf Nmap is available, its output is captured and returned. Otherwise, a built-in socket scanner is used as a fallback.  The socket scanner attempts to resolve the hostname. It iterates through defined ports, checking for connectivity. It captures service banners for common web ports. Any exceptions during socket operations are handled.\n\n\nThe port scan result report will include scanned port count, open port count, and a table of open ports with state, service, and banner information. If no ports are open, a corresponding message will be displayed. The report assesses attack surface, highlighting risks associated with specific ports like unencrypted protocols, exposed SMB, and externally accessible database ports. Recommendations include using TLS/SSH, auditing SMB configurations, and verifying database security.\n\n\nThe port scan tool generates a report showing open ports and associated services. The report highlights potential attack vectors based on port and service combinations, specifically noting risks such as unencrypted protocols or exposed databases. Recommendations for hardening the services are then included in the report.\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nThe vulnerability database tool provides a search function.  It uses the provided query to find matching CVE entries. If no matches are found, it offers suggestions on refining the search with CVE IDs or service names.\n\nThe search results, if any, will be formatted with up to five entries displayed, including CVE ID, title, service, CVSS score, CWE, affected versions, attack vector, and remediation. If more than five matches exist, it indicates the total number of additional advisories.\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nSQL injection test vectors are constructed, covering bypass, extraction, and time-based techniques. Each vector includes the payload and a description of the attack mechanism. Recommendations focus on utilizing parameterized queries.\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nA tool is created to generate various security audit payloads, including XSS and reverse shell attacks. XSS payloads utilize different contexts: image load failure, SVG inline events, DOM-based escapes, and JavaScript context escapes. Reverse shell templates are defined for bash, bash with redirection, python, and perl. The reverse shell generator expects IP:PORT input and allows specifying the target shell.\n\n\nReverse shell syntax templates are generated, targeting a specified IP and port, with examples for various shell types. Generated templates include bash, python, netcat (nc), PowerShell, and socat. Defensive hardening recommendations are included, emphasizing egress filtering, shell spawn restrictions, and process monitoring.\n\n\nCommand injection probes now include semicolon, shell chaining, subshell, and out-of-band DNS exfiltration tests.  Remediation focuses on avoiding shell invocation and validating inputs.\n\nServer-side template injection (SSTI) probes are defined for polyglot detection and RCE exploitation in Jinja2/Python and Twig/PHP.  Remediation involves not rendering user strings as templates.\n\nServer-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) probes are being examined.\n\n\nA set of probes for cloud metadata endpoints and localhost bypasses is defined.  Remediation steps involve URL validation against allowlists and blocking private IP address resolution.\n\nProbes for Local File Inclusion (LFI) and path traversal are constructed.  These include standard traversal techniques, URL encoded paths, and PHP wrappers. The proposed remediation uses path canonicalization and avoiding direct user input of file paths.\n\n\nThe `web_attack` tool receives a URL and a mode (headers, endpoints, or all). The tool audits HTTP security headers first. It attempts a connection, extracts response headers, and reports the status. Connection failures are handled, and error details are reported.\n\n\nThe \"web_attack\" tool checks the HTTP security headers of a given URL. It attempts a connection and retrieves response headers. The tool categorizes the headers into \"present\" and \"missing\" based on a predefined security header list and reports the status.  It also checks and reports potentially sensitive \"leakage\" headers.\n\n\nHTTP security header assessment is complete. Endpoint discovery will commence, starting with a predefined list of common probe paths against the target URL. The tool will iterate through a defined set of common endpoints to check their accessibility.\n\n\nEndpoint scanning is implemented, utilizing a predefined list of potentially sensitive paths for the audit. Requests are sent, and responses are analyzed to determine endpoint accessibility and status codes. Error handling is included. The report will categorize and describe findings.\n\n\nA new tool is registered for post-exploitation activities, targeting privilege escalation and auditing across Linux, Windows, and Active Directory environments. The tool will generate enumeration checklists, persistence detection rules, and facilitate lateral movement audits. I will begin examining this tool's implementation.\n\n\nLinux post-exploitation auditing commences with capability checks on binaries and examination of writable sensitive files.  Persistence mechanisms are being assessed via SSH keys, systemd services, shell configuration files, and cron jobs.  Lateral movement is investigated through network connections, ARP tables, and harvesting of SSH keys.\n\n\nPost-exploitation auditing on Linux systems now checks for capabilities on binaries and examines writeable, sensitive files. Assessment of persistence mechanisms includes SSH keys, systemd services, shell configurations, and cron jobs. The tool investigates lateral movement by examining network connections, ARP tables, and harvesting SSH keys.\n\nThe Windows post-exploitation tool is designed to audit privilege escalation by checking unquoted service paths, token privileges, AlwaysInstallElevated registry settings, and saved credentials. Persistence checks include registry run keys.\n\n\nWindows post-exploitation auditing encompasses scheduled tasks, startup folder examination, and accessibility backdoor analysis through registry queries.\n\nActive Directory post-exploitation activities include Kerberoasting and AS-REP roasting checks and pass-the-hash/ticket lateral movement assessment. Kerberoasting targets service accounts for offline password cracking, AS-REP roasting identifies accounts with disabled pre-authentication, and remediation involves Kerberos pre-authentication enforcement.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\k4ran\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\smartAGENT\\smartagent\\agent\\tools.py","EndLine":25,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing top lines of tools.py","toolSummary":"View top lines of tools.py"}}]}
